#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704980 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the rectangle $1\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le y\\le 3$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nUse Green's theorem:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=y^{2},\\\\Q&=- x y,\\\\P_y&=2 y,\\\\Q_x&=- y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(- y)-(2 y)\\\\&=- 3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is described by $1\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le y\\le 3$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/cd/cd02a65d414a04a3ba34acff123d597e306e8644e56d253b18a794d1745ef583.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704984 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the rectangle $-1\\le y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le x\\le 1$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nUse Green's theorem:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $x$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 y^{2},\\\\Q&=x y,\\\\P_y&=4 y,\\\\Q_x&=y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(y)-(4 y)\\\\&=- 3 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is described by $-1\\le y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le x\\le 1$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/0e/0e42550487fd743efb0b834e0fa05dfb9b51336d3b86aa8d1aa4815c4685792b.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186704985 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the rectangle $1\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le y\\le 2$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nUse Green's theorem:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(- y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- y^{2},\\\\Q&=0,\\\\P_y&=- 2 y,\\\\Q_x&=0,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(0)-(- 2 y)\\\\&=2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is described by $1\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le y\\le 2$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/33/33421b4259c4b180921212edf3e8f43aa97caed953720fa35846b51395f73cad.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705040 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the rectangle $1\\le y\\le 2,\\quad -1\\le x\\le 2$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nUse Green's theorem:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $x$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(- 2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 y^{2},\\\\Q&=x y,\\\\P_y&=- 4 y,\\\\Q_x&=y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(y)-(- 4 y)\\\\&=5 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is described by $1\\le y\\le 2,\\quad -1\\le x\\le 2$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/46/4696142d00d6a9e772d45c742ae69c1757003caec1f540860a92674065c93179.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(5 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705044 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the rectangle $1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le y\\le 4$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nUse Green's theorem:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(- 2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 y^{2},\\\\Q&=2 x y,\\\\P_y&=- 4 y,\\\\Q_x&=2 y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(2 y)-(- 4 y)\\\\&=6 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is described by $1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le y\\le 4$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/09/0917dbc5ce2f137f6daed3d18b2989806abd0cd2dba4ac32520df519a305bb26.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(6 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705048 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the rectangle $0\\le y\\le 2,\\quad -1\\le x\\le 2$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nUse Green's theorem:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $x$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(- 2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 y^{2},\\\\Q&=0,\\\\P_y&=- 4 y,\\\\Q_x&=0,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(0)-(- 4 y)\\\\&=4 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is described by $0\\le y\\le 2,\\quad -1\\le x\\le 2$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/5a/5a18db294405c464b4005bd189ae50858ba847edf184601e94250ffdac349b4c.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705052 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the rectangle $1\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le y\\le 1$. Its boundary $C$ is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nUse Green's theorem:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich repeated integral, integrating with respect to $y$ first, represents the following line integral? Use the actual region $D$ with bounds in increasing order. Do not evaluate it.\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\left(- y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The component functions are continuously differentiable on an open set containing $D$. For counterclockwise traversal, Green's theorem uses $Q_x-P_y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- y^{2},\\\\Q&=2 x y,\\\\P_y&=- 2 y,\\\\Q_x&=2 y,\\\\Q_x-P_y&=(2 y)-(- 2 y)\\\\&=4 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $D$ is described by $1\\le x\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le y\\le 1$. The bounds stay attached to their variables.\n\n![](images/31/31a3604e3309d583b5fb72ea986313759dd70d782f1d9d6fcd9954f58744c1c1.png)\n\nThe required setup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705275 :question/problem "Find all real values of $p$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ - 3 p - 4 & 4 p + 7 & - 3 p - 3 \\\\ - 5 p - 8 & 6 p + 13 & - 5 p - 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand along the first row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Compute the three signed contributions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1} & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}4 p + 7 & - 3 p - 3 \\\\ 6 p + 13 & - 5 p - 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)((4 p + 7)(- 5 p - 9)-(- 3 p - 3)(6 p + 13)) \\\\  & =(1)(- 2 p^{2} - 14 p - 24) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} - 14 p - 24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{2} & =-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}- 3 p - 4 & - 3 p - 3 \\\\ - 5 p - 8 & - 5 p - 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-2)((- 3 p - 4)(- 5 p - 9)-(- 3 p - 3)(- 5 p - 8)) \\\\  & =-(-2)(8 p + 12) \\\\  & =16 p + 24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{3} & =(2)\\begin{vmatrix}- 3 p - 4 & 4 p + 7 \\\\ - 5 p - 8 & 6 p + 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2)((- 3 p - 4)(6 p + 13)-(4 p + 7)(- 5 p - 8)) \\\\  & =(2)(2 p^{2} + 4 p + 4) \\\\  & =4 p^{2} + 8 p + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the signed contributions to obtain the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =T_1+T_2+T_3 \\\\  & =2 p^{2} + 10 p + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA singular matrix has determinant $0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 p^{2} + 10 p + 8 & =0 \\\\ 2 (p + 1) (p + 4) & =0 \\\\ p & =-4,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=-4$ and $p=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705285 :question/problem "Find all real values of $p$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}- p - 2 & - 2 p - 3 & p + 1 \\\\ 3 p + 3 & 4 p + 7 & - 3 p - 5 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand along the first row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Compute the three signed contributions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1} & =(- p - 2)\\begin{vmatrix}4 p + 7 & - 3 p - 5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(- p - 2)((4 p + 7)(2)-(- 3 p - 5)(-2)) \\\\  & =(- p - 2)(2 p + 4) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} - 8 p - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{2} & =-(- 2 p - 3)\\begin{vmatrix}3 p + 3 & - 3 p - 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(- 2 p - 3)((3 p + 3)(2)-(- 3 p - 5)(-1)) \\\\  & =-(- 2 p - 3)(3 p + 1) \\\\  & =6 p^{2} + 11 p + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{3} & =(p + 1)\\begin{vmatrix}3 p + 3 & 4 p + 7 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(p + 1)((3 p + 3)(-2)-(4 p + 7)(-1)) \\\\  & =(p + 1)(1 - 2 p) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} - p + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the signed contributions to obtain the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =T_1+T_2+T_3 \\\\  & =2 p^{2} + 2 p - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA singular matrix has determinant $0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 p^{2} + 2 p - 4 & =0 \\\\ 2 (p - 1) (p + 2) & =0 \\\\ p & =-2,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=-2$ and $p=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705286 :question/problem "Find all real values of $p$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 p - 1 & p & p + 2 \\\\ 2 p - 1 & 1 - p & - p \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand along the first row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Compute the three signed contributions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1} & =(- 2 p - 1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 - p & - p \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(- 2 p - 1)((1 - p)(2)-(- p)(1)) \\\\  & =(- 2 p - 1)(2 - p) \\\\  & =2 p^{2} - 3 p - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{2} & =-(p)\\begin{vmatrix}2 p - 1 & - p \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(p)((2 p - 1)(2)-(- p)(-2)) \\\\  & =-(p)(2 p - 2) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} + 2 p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{3} & =(p + 2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 p - 1 & 1 - p \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(p + 2)((2 p - 1)(1)-(1 - p)(-2)) \\\\  & =(p + 2)(1) \\\\  & =p + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the signed contributions to obtain the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =T_1+T_2+T_3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant is $0$ for every real value of $p$. Therefore, the matrix is singular for every real value of $p$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705287 :question/problem "Find all real values of $p$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 p & - p - 1 & 1 - p \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 p - 5 & 4 - p & 3 - p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand along the first row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Compute the three signed contributions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1} & =(2 p)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 - p & 3 - p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 p)((2)(3 - p)-(1)(4 - p)) \\\\  & =(2 p)(2 - p) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} + 4 p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{2} & =-(- p - 1)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 p - 5 & 3 - p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(- p - 1)((-2)(3 - p)-(1)(2 p - 5)) \\\\  & =-(- p - 1)(-1) \\\\  & =- p - 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{3} & =(1 - p)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 p - 5 & 4 - p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1 - p)((-2)(4 - p)-(2)(2 p - 5)) \\\\  & =(1 - p)(2 - 2 p) \\\\  & =2 p^{2} - 4 p + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the signed contributions to obtain the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =T_1+T_2+T_3 \\\\  & =1 - p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA singular matrix has determinant $0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 - p & =0 \\\\ - p & =-1 \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705362 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2-y$ that passes through the point $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the given point is normal to the level curve. First find the partial derivatives, treating the other variable as constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x, & f_y(x,y)&=4y-1,\\\\\n\\nabla f(x,y)&=2x\\,i+(4y-1)\\,j,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,1)&=2\\,i+3\\,j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\|\\nabla f(1,1)\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\nDivide by this magnitude to obtain a unit normal vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{\\nabla f(1,1)}{\\|\\nabla f(1,1)\\|}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(2\\,i+3\\,j).\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal vector. Of the displayed choices, only the vector above is both parallel to the gradient and of length one."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705363 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln(x+2y)-xy$ that passes through the point $(2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "At the point $(2,0)$, the logarithm has positive argument $2$, so the function is differentiable nearby. A nonzero gradient there is normal to the level curve.\n\nDifferentiate before substituting the coordinates. The chain rule for the logarithm and the derivatives of $-xy$ give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{x+2y}-y,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{2}{x+2y}-x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(2,0)&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-0\\right)i+\\left(\\frac{2}{2}-2\\right)j\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}i-j,\\\\\n\\|\\nabla f(2,0)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2+(-1)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormalize this nonzero vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}i-j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i-2\\,j).\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal vector. Omitting the derivatives of $-xy$ gives a different direction; keeping the gradient without normalization gives the wrong length."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705373 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=e^{x-y}\\sin(x+y)$ that passes through the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The function is differentiable everywhere. To find a normal vector to its level curve, compute the gradient at the specified point. Apply the product and chain rules to both partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=e^{x-y}\\sin(x+y)+e^{x-y}\\cos(x+y),\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=-e^{x-y}\\sin(x+y)+e^{x-y}\\cos(x+y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0\\right)$, use $\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{6}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0\\right)=\\frac{e^{\\pi/6}}{2}\\left((1+\\sqrt{3})\\,i+(\\sqrt{3}-1)\\,j\\right).\n$$\n\nThe common factor is positive, and this vector is nonzero. Its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\|\\nabla f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0\\right)\\right\\|\n&=\\frac{e^{\\pi/6}}{2}\\sqrt{(1+\\sqrt{3})^2+(\\sqrt{3}-1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{\\pi/6}}{2}\\sqrt{(4+2\\sqrt{3})+(4-2\\sqrt{3})}\\\\\n&=e^{\\pi/6}\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the gradient by its magnitude. The positive exponential factor cancels:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\left((1+\\sqrt{3})\\,i+(\\sqrt{3}-1)\\,j\\right).\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal vector. Both product-rule terms matter: differentiating only the sine factor would incorrectly give the direction $i+j$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705395 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2xy=x^2+3y^2-9.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Bring the terms to one side:\n\n$$\nx^2+3y^2-2xy-9=0.\n$$\n\nSet $f(x,y)=x^2+3y^2-2xy-9.$ The curve is the level set $f(x,y)=0.$ Its gradient at $(1,2)$ supplies a normal direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x-2y, & f_y(x,y)&=6y-2x,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,2)&=\\langle 2-4,12-2\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle -2,10\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line through $(1,2)$ parallel to $\\langle -2,10\\rangle$ has equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}&=\\frac{y-2}{10},\\\\\n10(x-1)&=-2(y-2),\\\\\n5x+y-7&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the normal line is $5x+y-7=0.$ Its slope is $-5.$ In contrast, the tangent line has slope $1/5$ and equation $-x+5y-9=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705399 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^2}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^2}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe curve is the level set $f(x,y)=1.$ Compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left\\langle \\frac{x-1}{2},\\frac{2(y+2)}{9}\\right\\rangle,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,1)&=\\left\\langle 0,\\frac{2}{3}\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero and points vertically. The normal line is therefore the vertical line through $(1,1):$\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nWe can also use the relation $b(x-x_0)=a(y-y_0)$ for a line parallel to $\\langle a,b\\rangle:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)&=0(y-1),\\\\\nx&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis form handles the zero first component without division by zero. The line $y=1$ is the horizontal tangent, whereas $x=0$ has the normal direction but misses the given point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705400 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2+2y^2=3.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nUse $f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2.$ The vector $\\nabla f(1,1)$ gives the direction of the normal line. For a direction vector $\\langle a,b\\rangle,$ a line through $(x_0,y_0)$ satisfies $b(x-x_0)=a(y-y_0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ Compute the partial derivatives, then evaluate the gradient at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x, & f_y(x,y)&=4y,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle 2,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this gradient is nonzero, it is a direction vector for the normal line. Using $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $(x_0,y_0)=(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x-1)&=2(y-1),\\\\\n2(x-1)&=y-1,\\\\\n2x-y-1&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the normal line is $2x-y-1=0.$ It passes through $(1,1)$ and has slope $2,$ the slope of the vector $\\langle 2,4\\rangle.$ The equation $x+2y-3=0$ instead describes the tangent line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705415 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=(x^2+y^2+z^2-1)^2.\n$$\n\nFind a nonzero normal vector at $P=(1,0,0)$ to the level surface of $f$ that passes through $P$." :question/worked-solution "First determine which level surface passes through $P$. Since $f(1,0,0)=0$, its equation is\n\n$$\n(x^2+y^2+z^2-1)^2=0.\n$$\n\nA real number has square zero exactly when it is zero, so this is the sphere\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2=1.\n$$\n\nDirect differentiation of the supplied function gives\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=4(x^2+y^2+z^2-1)(x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j+z\\,\\mathbf k),\n$$\n\nso $\\nabla f(P)=\\mathbf 0$. The zero vector has no direction and is not a normal vector. A zero gradient means that this defining function does not supply a normal through the nonzero-gradient rule; it does not mean that the surface has no normal.\n\nUse the equivalent defining function $g(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+z^2-1$ for the same surface. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x,y,z)&=2x\\,\\mathbf i+2y\\,\\mathbf j+2z\\,\\mathbf k,\\\\\n\\nabla g(1,0,0)&=2\\,\\mathbf i\\ne\\mathbf 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus every nonzero multiple of $\\mathbf i$ is normal here. The listed vector $-3\\,\\mathbf i=-\\frac{3}{2}\\nabla g(P)$ is a normal vector. The vectors $\\mathbf j$ and $\\mathbf k$ are tangent directions, and $\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j$ is not parallel to the normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705419 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2+xy+z^2$, find a vector perpendicular to the level surface that passes through $P=(1,-1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient at $P$ is a normal vector to the level surface through $P$. First compute the three partial derivatives, treating the other variables as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=2x+y, & f_y&=x, & f_z&=2z. \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf i+x\\,\\mathbf j+2z\\,\\mathbf k,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,-1,2)&=(2(1)-1)\\,\\mathbf i+1\\,\\mathbf j+2(2)\\,\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector is nonzero, so $\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705422 :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\nx^2+xy+z^2=y+2\n$$\n\nat $P=(1,1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write the surface as a level surface by moving all terms to one side:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=x^2+xy+z^2-y-2.\n$$\n\nThe surface has equation $F(x,y,z)=0$. The point lies on it because $F(1,1,1)=1+1+1-1-2=0$. Compute the gradient of the whole defining function, including the term $-y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla F(x,y,z)&=(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x-1)\\,\\mathbf j+2z\\,\\mathbf k,\\\\\n\\nabla F(1,1,1)&=3\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. Every nonzero scalar multiple of it is also normal to the surface. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla F(1,1,1)=-6\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-6\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf k$ is the listed normal vector. Differentiating only the left side would incorrectly leave a $\\mathbf j$ component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705432 :question/problem "The surface $x^2+2y^2-z=0$ contains the point $P(1,-1,3)$. For $F(x,y,z)=x^2+2y^2-z$, you are given\n\n$$\n\\nabla F(1,-1,3)=\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWhich vector equation describes the normal line to the surface at $P$? Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient of a level function is perpendicular to its level surface. Thus $\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle$ is a direction vector for the normal line.\n\nThe position vector of $P$ is $\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle$. A line through $P$ with direction vector $\\mathbf n$ has equation $\\mathbf r=\\mathbf p+t\\mathbf n$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ this gives $P$. The point supplies the constant vector, and the gradient supplies the direction vector; they have different roles."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705447 :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $e^{xy}+z^2=x+1$ at the point $(1,0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Move the variable term $x$ to the left and set\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=e^{xy}+z^2-x.\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=1$. Using the chain rule for $e^{xy}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\nabla F(x,y,z)=\\langle ye^{xy}-1,\\ xe^{xy},\\ 2z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nAt $P=(1,0,1)$, we have $e^{xy}=e^0=1$, so\n\n$$\n\\nabla F(1,0,1)=\\langle 0(1)-1,\\ 1(1),\\ 2(1)\\rangle=\\langle -1,1,2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero and supplies the direction of the normal line. Thus a vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,0,1\\rangle+t\\langle -1,1,2\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nWriting $x=1-t$, $y=t$, and $z=1+2t$ and eliminating $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-1}=\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n1-x=y=\\frac{z-1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705448 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=x^2+xy+2y^2$ at $P(1,-1,2)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring all variable terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=x^2+xy+2y^2-z.\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=0$. Its gradient is\n\n$$\n\\nabla F(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x+y,\\ x+4y,\\ -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla F(1,-1,2)&=\\langle 2(1)-1,\\ 1+4(-1),\\ -1\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 1,-3,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis nonzero vector is normal to the surface. Using $P$ as the base point, the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-3,-1\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nThe constant vector gives $P$ at $t=0$, while the coefficient of $t$ gives the normal direction. Multiplying all components of that direction by the same nonzero scalar would describe the same line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705474 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at $(2,1)$ if $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2} - 2 y$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to one coordinate at a time, holding the other constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=6 x+2 y+0+0 \\\\ &=6 x + 2 y \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=0+2 x+4 y-2 \\\\ &=2 x + 4 y - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=(6 x + 2 y)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+(2 x + 4 y - 2)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(2,1)&=6 \\left(2\\right) + 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=14 \\\\ f_y(2,1)&=2 \\left(2\\right) + 4 \\left(1\\right) - 2 \\\\ &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(2,1)=\\langle 14,\\,6\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705475 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at $(2,3)$ if $f(x,y)=y^{2} \\sqrt{x + 7}$. Work on the domain $x + 7>0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to one coordinate at a time, holding the other constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=y^{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x + 7}} \\\\ &=\\frac{y^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{x + 7}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 y\\sqrt{x + 7} \\\\ &=2 y \\sqrt{x + 7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{x + 7}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+(2 y \\sqrt{x + 7})\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(2,3)&=\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right) + 7}} \\\\ &=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ f_y(2,3)&=2 \\left(3\\right) \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right) + 7} \\\\ &=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(2,3)=\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\,18\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705477 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=- 2 y^{4} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}$. Work on the domain $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to one coordinate at a time, holding the other constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- y \\cos{\\left(y x \\right)}- 2 y^{4}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ &=- y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{2 y^{4}}{x} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x \\cos{\\left(y x \\right)}- 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} \\\\ &=- x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=(- y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{2 y^{4}}{x})\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+(- x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)})\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705486 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{- 3 x - 3 y}{- x + y}$. Work on the domain $y - x\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(-3)(y - x)-(- 3 y - 3 x)(-1)}{(y - x)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{6 y}{\\left(- x + y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(-3)(y - x)-(- 3 y - 3 x)\\cdot1}{(y - x)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{6 x}{\\left(- x + y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(- \\frac{6 y}{\\left(- x + y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{6 x}{\\left(- x + y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705498 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at $(-2,1)$ if $f(x,y)=e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to one coordinate at a time, holding the other constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- ye^{- y^{2} - y x - 1} \\\\ &=- y e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=(- 2 y - x)e^{- y^{2} - y x - 1} \\\\ &=\\left(- x - 2 y\\right) e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=(- y e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1})\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+(\\left(- x - 2 y\\right) e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1})\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-2,1)&=- \\left(1\\right) e^{- \\left(-2\\right) \\left(1\\right) - \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 1} \\\\ &=-1 \\\\ f_y(-2,1)&=\\left(- \\left(-2\\right) - 2 \\left(1\\right)\\right) e^{- \\left(-2\\right) \\left(1\\right) - \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 1} \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(-2,1)=\\langle -1,\\,0\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705513 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2} y - 2 y^{2}}{x - y}$. Work on the domain $x - y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(6 x y)(x - y)-(3 x^{2} y - 2 y^{2})\\cdot1}{(x - y)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{y \\left(3 x^{2} - 6 x y + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(3 x^{2} - 4 y)(x - y)-(3 x^{2} y - 2 y^{2})(-1)}{(x - y)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x y + 2 y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{y \\left(3 x^{2} - 6 x y + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x y + 2 y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705515 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{2 x^{2} - 2 x y^{2}}{x + y}$. Work on the domain $y + x\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(- 2 y^{2} + 4 x)(y + x)-(- 2 y^{2} x + 2 x^{2})(1)}{(y + x)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 \\left(- x^{2} - 2 x y + y^{3}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(- 4 y x)(y + x)-(- 2 y^{2} x + 2 x^{2})\\cdot1}{(y + x)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 x \\left(2 x y + x + y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(- \\frac{2 \\left(- x^{2} - 2 x y + y^{3}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{2 x \\left(2 x y + x + y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705522 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{2 x^{2} y + 3 y^{2}}{x + 2 y}$. Work on the domain $x + 2 y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(4 x y)(x + 2 y)-(2 x^{2} y + 3 y^{2})\\cdot1}{(x + 2 y)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{y \\left(2 x^{2} + 8 x y - 3 y\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(2 x^{2} + 6 y)(x + 2 y)-(2 x^{2} y + 3 y^{2})(2)}{(x + 2 y)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{y \\left(2 x^{2} + 8 x y - 3 y\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705525 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{- x^{2} y - y^{2}}{x - y}$. Work on the domain $x - y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(- 2 x y)(x - y)-(- x^{2} y - y^{2})\\cdot1}{(x - y)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{y \\left(x^{2} - 2 x y - y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(- x^{2} - 2 y)(x - y)-(- x^{2} y - y^{2})(-1)}{(x - y)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x^{3} + 2 x y - y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(- \\frac{y \\left(x^{2} - 2 x y - y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{x^{3} + 2 x y - y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705560 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ given $f(x,y,z)=e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=- 2 y e^{- 2 y x} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ &=- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=- 2 x e^{- 2 y x} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ &=- 2 x e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=2 z e^{- 2 y x} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ &=2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ - 2 x e^{- 2 x y} \\sin{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 2 z e^{- 2 x y} \\cos{\\left(z^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705577 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ at $(2,2,3)$ given $f(x,y,z)=\\ln{\\left(\\sqrt{x z \\left(y - 1\\right)} \\right)}.$ Work where $z>0$, $x>0$, $y - 1>0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{z \\left(y - 1\\right) \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}}}{\\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 x} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{z x \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}}}{\\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 \\left(y - 1\\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{x \\left(y - 1\\right) \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}}}{\\sqrt{z x \\left(y - 1\\right)}} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{2 x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2 \\left(y - 1\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2 z}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=2$, $y=2$, $z=3$ into these partial derivatives.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(2,2,3)&=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f_{y}(2,2,3)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f_{z}(2,2,3)&=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(2,2,3)&=\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705583 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ given $f(x,y,z)=x z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}.$ Work where $z>0$, $x>0$, $y>0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{z x}{y} \\\\ &=\\frac{x z}{y} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}z \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{x z}{y} \\\\ x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705588 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ at $(-2,2,-1)$ given $f(x,y,z)=- x^{2} \\left(z + 1\\right) + 2 x y + y^{2} \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=- 2 \\left(z + 1\\right) x + 2 y \\\\ &=- 2 x \\left(z + 1\\right) + 2 y \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} y + 2 x \\\\ &=2 x + 2 y \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=2 \\left(z + 1\\right) y^{2} - x^{2} \\\\ &=- x^{2} + 2 y^{2} \\left(z + 1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 x \\left(z + 1\\right) + 2 y \\\\ 2 x + 2 y \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ - x^{2} + 2 y^{2} \\left(z + 1\\right)\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=-2$, $y=2$, $z=-1$ into these partial derivatives.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(-2,2,-1)&=4 \\\\ f_{y}(-2,2,-1)&=-4 \\\\ f_{z}(-2,2,-1)&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,2,-1)&=\\begin{pmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705590 :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ given $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}}}.$ Work where $x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} > 0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other two coordinates constant in each partial derivative. Apply the power, chain, or product rule to the factors that depend on the active coordinate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=- \\frac{2 x}{2 \\left(\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + z^{2} + x^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(y - 1\\right)}{2 \\left(\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + z^{2} + x^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{y - 1}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=- \\frac{2 z}{2 \\left(\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + z^{2} + x^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ &=- \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partials in $x,y,z$ order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\begin{pmatrix}- \\frac{x}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{y - 1}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} \\\\ - \\frac{z}{\\left(x^{2} + z^{2} + \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\end{pmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705597 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=e^{x - y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705614 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- e^{x - y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705616 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- e^{- x - y} \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} - 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}]}
